# Footer

<h2 id="purpose">Purpose</h2>

A footer holds supporting links at the end of a page: accessibility, help, privacy or licensing. Use it on the documentation site or on a separate service page, not on a dashboard. Home Assistant has no page footer, and a website footer does not belong in every card or in the mobile app.

This page owns the page footer. The header and footer areas of a Home Assistant card are owned by [Card Header & Footer Widgets](card-header-and-footer-widgets.html). For moving between pages, see [Navigation](navigation.html).

This page covers:

- Footer
  - `<footer>`

<h2 id="anatomy">Anatomy</h2>

- The footer element, at the end of the page.
- A navigation with its own label, such as “Footer”, holding the links.
- Optional short text, such as the name of the guide.

<h2 id="variants">Variants, variables and extensibility</h2>

<h3 id="child-footer"><code>&lt;footer&gt;</code></h3>

A standard HTML element. When it belongs to the whole page it is a page landmark that screen readers can jump to. Inside an article or a section it is not a landmark. A footer cannot contain another footer or a header.

Home Assistant has no page footer for you to style. Use this element on Civica pages and in custom cards you write. The footer inside Civica’s own card is part of that card and is owned by the card pages.

Label the navigation inside the footer, for example `aria-label="Footer"`, so it is told apart from other navigation on the page.

The Civica theme sets no variable for the footer. Links use `primary-color` (#1D70B8 in light mode, #C0D7EB in dark mode), borders use `divider-color` (#CECECE in light mode, #50565A in dark mode), and the focus colour is #FFDD00. The documentation footer has a light grey background and a top border, with links in a row that wraps.

**The documentation footer.** It states permanently that Civica is an independent project, gives the licence for the code and for the guidance, and links to [Licence](../community/licence.html) for the full terms and attribution. The statement stays after the first-visit notice is hidden. Keep such a statement to a short sentence, let it wrap on narrow screens rather than shrinking the text, and link the licence name to the full terms.

<h2 id="limits">Advantages and limitations</h2>

- A footer is a predictable place for help and information links.
- A page footer is a landmark, so screen reader users can jump to it, but landmark behaviour varies.
- It is the last thing a keyboard user reaches, so it is a poor place for anything critical.
- A large footer with several columns needs a sensible reading order when it stacks.

<h2 id="usage">Usage policy and cognitive accessibility</h2>

Put the same links, in the same order, on every page. Use plain link names such as “Accessibility” and “Help”. Do not put device controls in a footer.

Show the name and licence that belong to the page. The documentation uses Civica. A separate household or business page may use its own name and artwork, if it has the right to. An app’s own settings or About screen may be the better home for the same information.

Do not promise a support channel or a response time that you cannot provide.

Success criteria that apply:

- 1.3.1 Info and Relationships: the footer and its navigation are marked up as such.
- 2.4.1 Bypass Blocks and 2.4.5 Multiple Ways: people can get to the footer links and the main content.
- 2.4.4 Link Purpose (In Context): each link name says where it goes.
- 3.2.3 Consistent Navigation and 3.2.6 Consistent Help: the links and help are in the same place on every page.
- 2.4.7 Focus Visible and 2.4.11 Focus Not Obscured (Minimum): the focus can be seen.
- 2.5.8 Target Size (Minimum): links have enough space between them.
- 1.4.10 Reflow and 1.4.12 Text Spacing: the footer wraps at 320 pixels and survives wider spacing.

The same links in the same place every time keep the load low.

<h3 id="check">Check it works</h3>

Test the last keyboard stops, zoom, how the links wrap on a small screen, and every link. See [Test the interface](../accessibility/testing.html).

